{"candidates":[{"claim":"The safest autonomous protocol is preserve-then-reclaim gated by a Byzantine quorum of signed null-attestations from an anti-entropy mesh, ensuring no completed evidence hash can be overlooked.","counterconditions":["A network partition may split the mesh such that a minority witnesses the evidence while the majority issues null-attestations","The evidence hash may have been generated but not yet propagated to any mesh node at the moment of crash","Compromised witness keys may allow forged null-attestations that falsely authorize reclamation"],"mechanism":"Upon detecting a crash without terminal record, the protocol seals the workspace and broadcasts a reclamation intent to a mesh of witness nodes. Each node scans its local evidence namespace for any record matching the workspace's problem fingerprint and returns either a positive evidence hash or a signed null-attestation. A deterministic decision rule collects these responses and permits reclamation only upon receiving a Byzantine-fault-tolerant quorum of null-attestations. The protocol durably records the reclamation intent, the full set of signed attestations, the quorum threshold, and the final decision with a monotonic epoch to prevent replay.","optional_refs":["621d11070af80e03e5efd17a99129c89144bf1f35b94f043cfc7e5fe4ed5ef00","5e55f1ec401b22d793ce3cec1a2faa5d4b27f331796cae29a3b37b65438f2a1f","8cefbd0287a5f52f7a7cc58513b4b9896a13d0cb694f27e0175373eb587ecaf3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"No autonomous reclamation protocol can guarantee never to silently displace completed evidence, because the absence of a terminal record is observationally equivalent to both incomplete execution and completed-but-lost execution; an external adjudicator must render the verdict.","counterconditions":["The external adjudicator may be unavailable, introducing unbounded reclamation delay","The checkpoint chain may omit the precise state transition where evidence was finalized","The evidence namespace may contain entries that are semantically valid but not causally linked to this workspace"],"mechanism":"The protocol durably records deterministic checkpoint hashes of the workspace at regular intervals alongside the problem input specification. All completed evidence is published to an external evidence namespace with strong durability. After crash, the workspace is quarantined rather than reclaimed. An external adjudicator compares the checkpoint chain, the evidence namespace, and the problem input to render a binding verdict on whether evidence was completed. Reclamation is permitted only upon explicit adjudicator signature.","optional_refs":["8cefbd0287a5f52f7a7cc58513b4b9896a13d0cb694f27e0175373eb587ecaf3","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","bf0cfa661176c31beec483e5bbe68f0efdc3e5f370c6850ea595ece81a63f059"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"Silent displacement is structurally impossible if the workspace is replaced by a Merkle-DAG of pending side effects where completed evidence is defined as a content-addressed node replicated to a threshold of anchor nodes; reclamation is merely garbage collection of unreached leaves.","counterconditions":["Correlated crashes of all anchor nodes before replication completes may destroy the only copies of the evidence node","A content-addressed collision may cause a false linkage","Non-deterministic oracle inputs not captured in the DAG edges may make replay diverge from the original execution"],"mechanism":"The workspace is modeled as an append-only causal log of deterministic transformations, each yielding a content-addressed node. Evidence completion is the act of linking the evidence node to the problem trunk and achieving a threshold of anti-entropy anchors. The protocol durably records only the problem trunk root hash and the anchor node IDs. Crash recovery replays from the trunk root. Reclamation removes only orphan nodes that are not linked to any trunk, which by construction excludes all completed evidence.","optional_refs":["5e55f1ec401b22d793ce3cec1a2faa5d4b27f331796cae29a3b37b65438f2a1f","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"claim":"The safest protocol is a consensus lease that treats workspace allocation as a distributed lock released only by publishing a completed evidence hash to a BFT ledger, and requires a signed absence certificate for reclamation after crash.","counterconditions":["A view change during crash may cause the evidence hash to be accepted by a minority that is later excluded from the supermajority","Premature lease epoch reuse may collide with in-flight evidence publications","A temporarily reduced quorum may issue an absence certificate despite hidden evidence"],"mechanism":"Workspace allocation writes a lease record (workspace_id, problem_digest, epoch) to a BFT ledger. The holder must periodically renew. Completion appends the evidence hash and atomically releases the lease. If the process crashes without terminal record, the lease remains active. Reclamation requires a special absence certificate signed by a supermajority of consensus participants affirming they have not accepted an evidence hash for this lease epoch. The protocol durably records the lease entry, all renewal attempts, and the signed absence certificate.","optional_refs":["8cefbd0287a5f52f7a7cc58513b4b9896a13d0cb694f27e0175373eb587ecaf3","bf0cfa661176c31beec483e5bbe68f0efdc3e5f370c6850ea595ece81a63f059","621d11070af80e03e5efd17a99129c89144bf1f35b94f043cfc7e5fe4ed5ef00"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"claim":"If the workspace is strictly deterministic with respect to its inputs, the safety guarantee collapses to idempotency: the protocol must durably record the full entropy tape and external oracle log, making any displaced evidence recoverable by deterministic replay rather than preventing displacement.","counterconditions":["Entropy sources such as thermal noise may be physically unrecordable","Replay cost may exceed practical time bounds, making evidence logically recoverable but operationally lost","External oracle state may drift since the original query, causing replay to produce different evidence"],"mechanism":"The workspace is a pure function of the problem input plus consumed entropy. The protocol durably records the problem input hash, a sequential log of all external random seeds and oracle responses, and intermediate Merkle checkpoints. Crash without terminal record triggers automatic deterministic replay from the last checkpoint. The scratch workspace can be safely reclaimed because evidence exists either in the durable intent log or is reconstructible. The protocol never silently displaces evidence because evidence is not located in the ephemeral workspace.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"claim":"The safest protocol separates durability classes: an append-only intent log with strong durability for metadata, and a weakly durable scratch workspace. Reclamation is safe because evidence only exists upon pairing an intent record with a completion certificate in the durable log.","counterconditions":["An acknowledged completion certificate write may be lost due to a subsequent log corruption","Timestamp or sequence ordering failures may pair an intent with the wrong certificate","A workspace may contain evidence that is valid but not yet certified at crash time"],"mechanism":"Before computation, the process appends an intent record (problem_id, input_hash, lease_nonce) to a strongly durable, single-writer append-only log. The workspace itself is treated as weakly durable scratch. Upon completion, an immutable evidence artifact is written to a separate strongly durable store, and a completion certificate is appended to the intent log. The protocol durably records the intent log and all completion certificates. A crash leaves an unpaired intent; the scratch workspace may be reclaimed immediately because the absence of a completion certificate proves no evidence was durably completed.","optional_refs":["621d11070af80e03e5efd17a99129c89144bf1f35b94f043cfc7e5fe4ed5ef00","8cefbd0287a5f52f7a7cc58513b4b9896a13d0cb694f27e0175373eb587ecaf3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[{"because":"NEW_002 dissolves the safety tension raised in NEW_001 by removing mutability; NEW_001 exposes the hidden-oracle risk that makes NEW_002's dissolution conditional.","direction":"symmetric","from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"The workspace is strictly deterministic and per-problem scoped.","relation_hint":"Interdependency between determinism-as-idempotency and epistemic-status reframe.","to_ref":"NEW_002","weakens_when":"Hidden oracle inputs or cross-problem state invalidate strict determinism."}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Speculative reframe: 'terminal record' is an epistemic status, not a physical object. If the process and problem are strictly deterministic, the correct evidence exists implicitly in the mathematical solution space; the crash merely destroys a physical token. This implies that 'displacement' is only meaningful under resource constraints that make recomputation impossible or under hidden non-determinism that makes the token irreplaceable.","possible_next_move":"Derive the minimal durability requirement if evidence is purely logical: only the problem input need be preserved.","unfinished":"Unresolved whether the problem assumes evidence is a physical token or a logical proposition.","why_keep_this":"Tests whether the problem's safety requirement is fundamentally about epistemic preservation or merely about cache eviction policy."},"local_key":"NEW_001"},{"body":{"content":"Counterfactual contradiction: The problem states 'deterministic per-problem workspace,' which suggests the workspace is a pure function scoped to a single problem. If so, the workspace is definitionally idempotent and reclaimable, and the entire question of 'safest reclamation' is a red herring. The true risk is only if the workspace is mischaracterized as deterministic when it actually ingests problem-external entropy or accumulates cross-problem state.","possible_next_move":"Map all hidden non-determinism sources (wall-clock, RNG, I/O) that would invalidate the idempotency assumption.","why_keep_this":"Directly challenges the problem's premise by dissolving the tension if determinism is taken literally; useful for testing boundary conditions."},"local_key":"NEW_002"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Does 'deterministic per-problem workspace' logically preclude the ingestion of wall-clock oracles or physical entropy? If yes, then evidence displacement is always recoverable by replay and the search for a 'safest' protocol is moot; if no, then the determinism claim is false and the safety guarantee must target oracle-state preservation.","related_refs":["NEW_001","NEW_002"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}